<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.5: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel or Perpendicular to an Axis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00315</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.5: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel or Perpendicular to an Axis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>df6576fd-7a74-4134-8df3-a31cee00a748</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>The line \( x = 5 \) and the point \( (3, -2) \) are shown on the graph.</para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_12_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <para>Work with a partner to complete the tasks:</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Identify the slope of the original line, \( l \).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Undefined</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph line
    \(m\) through the point (3, -2) and parallel to line \(l\) using the Desmos tool below. </item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_12_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>Then,</para>
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Identify the slope of the new line, \( m \).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Undefined</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the equation of the new line, \( m \).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( x = 3 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph line
    \( n\) through the point \((3, -2)\) and perpendicular to line \(l\) using the Desmos tool below.</item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_16_12_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>Then,</para>
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Identify the slope of the new line, \( n \).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>0</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the equation of the new line, \( n \).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( y = -2 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para>The original line \(l\) and the line \(m\) that you created are both parallel to the \(y\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis. It follows, then, that line n is parallel to the \(x\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis.</para>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the equation of a line that is parallel to the \(y\)-axis and passing through \((-2, -5)\).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( x = -2 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#9-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the equation of a line that is perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis and passing through \((1, 5)\).</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( y = 5 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#10-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the equation of a line parallel to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((3, 4)\). </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\( y = 4 \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#11-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>How does the line described as “parallel to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((3, 4)\)” relate to the \(y\)-axis? Will it always, sometimes, or never be perpendicular to it?
      </para>
          <newline/>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Always perpendicular.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
